{"t":1795468825,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"710670dc-bd66-4207-a99e-21986fcd4d2b","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-23T19:57:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 231957\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n257 PM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nIssued Gale Watches for late Wednesday night through about\nmidday Friday. Models are tending to lean toward a heavier \nrainfall this Thanksgiving Day with a chance for a few rumbles \nof thunder with an approaching coastal low pressure system from \nthe south. Winds will also increase rapidly Wednesday night and \ncontinue through the day Thanksgiving.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Cooler through mid week.\n\n- 2) A coastal low pressure system will bring the potential for\n  heavy rain, damaging winds, and coastal flooding, as well as a \n  little snow and ice in the mountains late Wednesday through \n  Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler through mid week.\n\nA notable change in the pattern takes place this evening as a \nsecondary surge arrives. This ushers in a period of brisk winds \nalong with falling temperatures and dew points. The result is a \nreturn of seasonably cold overnight conditions. Tonight's lows \nwill mainly be in the 30s east of the Blue Ridge, with mid/upper\n20s off to the west.\n\nThe temperature forecast for Tuesday and Wednesday are almost \nmirror images of one another. Daily highs range from the mid 40s\nto mid 50s, locally a bit cooler in the mountains. Between the \ntwo days, Tuesday will provide ample sunshine as a broad surface\nhigh tracks from the Midwest. By Tuesday night, a stout 1042-mb\nridge sits across New England which will make for a cold night \nover the local area. Forecast lows fall into the mid teens to \nmid 20s. For those along and east of I-95, upper 20s to near \nfreezing temperatures are more likely. Heading into Wednesday, \nskies will cloud up through the day ahead of the potent \nThanksgiving Day system. Any impacts largely hold off until \nWednesday night as northeasterly winds pick up in earnest. \nAlthough timing varies between models, gusty showers are \nforecast to lift northward across the area overnight. For more \ninformation on this storm, check out Key Message 2 (below).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal low pressure system will bring the \npotential for heavy rain, damaging winds, and coastal flooding,\nas well as a little snow and ice in the mountains late \nWednesday through Friday.\n\nA coastal low pressure system near the Southeast U.S. Coast \nWednesday night will become more developed and intensify as it \nmoves northward toward the Mid-Atlantic for Thanksgiving Day \ninto early Friday. The impacts of the coastal low pressure \nsystem will depend on the exact track of the coastal low, the \nconnection to the upper-level low in support, and timing moving \nup the coast. The general trends in the last few model cycles \nhave been slower, stronger, and a bit further west, but still \nwith moderate spread.\n\nLight to moderate rain should move into southern parts of our region \novernight Wednesday into early Thanksgiving as the coastal low is \nover the eastern Carolinas. Increasing northeast to easterly winds\nwill also occur overnight Wednesday and become quite gusty heading\ninto Thanksgiving Day. As the winds remain strong to perhaps \ndamaging, rainfall could become a prolonged period of moderate \nto heavy rainfall on Thanksgiving. The origin of part of this \nsystem in the tropical East Pacific into the Caribbean along \nwith strong to extreme moisture transport in intense onshore \nlow-level winds approaching 80 knots may lead to very efficient\nrainfall rates despite limited instability (MUCAPE less than \n250 J/kg). The heaviest rain would most likely occur as the \ncoastal low moves out of eastern North Carolina and into \nsoutheastern Virginia. It would be at this point in time that \neverything is occurring all at once...strong low pressure \nnearby, moderate to heavy rainfall leading to 1 to 3 inches \ntotal, sustained winds of 15 to 30 mph gusting 35 to 50 mph or \nso, minor to moderate coastal flooding along the western shores \nof the Chesapeake Bay and the tidal Potomac River, and slightly \nabove average temperatures.\n\nMuch of the rainfall should taper or come to an end from southwest \nto northeast, winds diminishing and becoming more southwesterly as \nthe low pressure exits, and temperatures start to fall gradually. \nCoastal flooding may still be a factor as it may take a little more \ntime to recede once the low exits.\n\nA little rain and snow mix, or light snow may unfold overnight \nThursday into Friday over the Alleghenies as the secondary low \npressure system moves in place of the coastal low and starts to \npush out to the east. It's a little early to see how much snow \naccumulation could occur, but with the unfavorable (south-of-\nwest) wind direction, upper low support, and colder air, there \nshould be some accumulation on the mountaintops.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nWhile a few showers are possible (mainly near/south of DCA and\neast of CHO) through this evening, do not anticipate much in the\nway of restrictions. Expect prevailing northwesterlies with \ngusts picking up to around 20 knots through this evening. \n\nVFR conditions continue into Tuesday and Wednesday as stout high\npressure builds to the north. Winds in turn become northerly \nfor Tuesday before shifting to northeasterly Tuesday night into \nWednesday. Any sub-VFR conditions should hold off until \nWednesday night as a potent system impacts the region into \nThanksgiving Day. Overnight northeasterly gusts begin to \napproach 25 to 30 knots ahead of daybreak.\n\nIFR to LIFR conditions seem more probable at the terminals with\nthe approaching coastal low from the south by Thursday morning.\nBoth wind and rain combinations could drop ceilings and \nvisibility quickly or even more persistently Thursday into \nThursday evening. VFR conditions should develop moreso later \nThursday evening through early Friday, as precipitation tapers \nand ends and winds shift and diminish. Winds northeast to east \n20 to 25 knots gusts 30 to 40 knots Thanksgiving Day and into \nthe evening. Winds becoming southwest and diminishing 15 to 20 \nknots gusts up to 25 knots Thursday night and Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nA stronger north to northwesterly surge moves in this evening \nwith gusts increasing to 20 to 30 knots into tonight and the \nfirst half of Tuesday. The northerly winds remain elevated \nthrough the remainder of Tuesday before winds turn more \nnortheasterly by Wednesday. Additional advisories will likely be\nneeded through Wednesday. Ahead of the strong system arriving \nfor Thanksgiving Day, northeasterlies begin to approach near \ngale levels late Wednesday night.\n\nGale Watches are in effect late Wednesday night through midday \nFriday. Can't rule out at least a brief period of storm-force\ngusts mainly over the middle Chesapeake Bay on Thursday. Small \nCraft Advisory conditions likely into Friday afternoon. Winds \nnortheast to east 25 to 35 knots gusts up to 45 knots Thursday.\nWinds becoming southeast then southwest 15 to 25 knots gusts up\nto 30 knots Thursday night into Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWinds gradually pick up in intensity this evening into the \nnight. This brings in cooler weather for the week, but also \nlower daytime humidities on Tuesday. A stronger coastal system \nis expected to bring wet and windy conditions to the region \nWednesday into Thanksgiving Day. There is increasing confidence \nthis brings wetting rains to the entire area. Gusty winds \npersist into Friday before high pressure approaches from the \nwest by the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nElevated tides will begin to subside this evening in northwest\nflow. However, astronomical tides are very high with the\nupcoming full moon on Tuesday. This could result in near minor\nflooding along vulnerable shoreline through early evening.\n\nA strong coastal low pressure area will move up the Eastern \nSeaboard Wednesday into Thanksgiving Day. Depending on the \nevolution and track, tide levels may rise once again. Model \nprogs still vary widely from abnormally low tides to moderate \ntidal flooding due to uncertainty in exact wind direction/\nmagnitude, which will depend on the track of the low.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 8 PM this evening to 1 PM EST \n     Tuesday for ANZ530>532-535-536-538>540-542.\n     Gale Watch from late Wednesday night through Friday afternoon \n     for ANZ530>543.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 8 PM this evening to 6 PM EST \n     Tuesday for ANZ533-534-537-541-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/KLW/DHOF\nAVIATION...BRO/KLW/DHOF\nMARINE...BRO/KLW/DHOF\nFIRE WEATHER...LWX\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...LWX\n"},"forecast":{"t":1795468748,"days":{"2026-11-23":{"hi":71,"lo":40,"pop":18,"short":"Isolated Rain Showers"},"2026-11-24":{"hi":53,"lo":32,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-25":{"hi":54,"lo":44,"pop":58,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-26":{"hi":62,"lo":47,"pop":99,"short":"Rain"},"2026-11-27":{"hi":58,"lo":40,"pop":39,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-28":{"hi":57,"lo":39,"pop":3,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-29":{"hi":61,"lo":41,"pop":10,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1795467600,"n":48,"ceil":[6600,6100,6000,5700,5700,5700,6100,6800,7000,8700,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,11100,12300,13600,13600,13600],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10